1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,280
Today, we're going to cover the basics of instruction set architectures, or ISAs.

2
00:00:08,280 --> 00:00:14,120
As you learned in 252 and 354, instructions are the words of the computer, and the ISA

3
00:00:14,120 --> 00:00:18,120
is the vocabulary that the processor understands.

4
00:00:18,120 --> 00:00:22,960
The ISA defines the full extent of what software can tell the processor to do.

5
00:00:22,960 --> 00:00:24,460
Well, almost.

6
00:00:24,460 --> 00:00:27,960
We'll cover those later in the course.

7
00:00:27,960 --> 00:00:33,380
The important part about ISAs is that they abstract away the implementation.

8
00:00:33,380 --> 00:00:38,420
Intel's x86 backwards compatibility means that an ad instruction will produce identical

9
00:00:38,420 --> 00:00:42,620
results across multiple generations of processors.

10
00:00:42,620 --> 00:00:47,540
If you still have a desktop PC, you could replace the processor with the latest generation

11
00:00:47,540 --> 00:00:52,420
and your operating system would still boot and your applications would still execute,

12
00:00:52,420 --> 00:00:57,540
just hopefully a little faster and consuming a little less power than before.

13
00:00:57,540 --> 00:01:01,460
Old binaries probably aren't aware of the new instructions, so you might not realize

14
00:01:01,460 --> 00:01:06,780
the full power of the new silicon.

15
00:01:06,780 --> 00:01:11,820
We're going to focus on MIPS and a MIPS like ISA in this class because it's simple, sensible,

16
00:01:11,820 --> 00:01:13,220
and regular.

17
00:01:13,220 --> 00:01:18,380
There aren't many instruction variants that cause problems with implementations.

18
00:01:18,380 --> 00:01:23,180
Another version of our textbook uses an open source ISA called risk five.

19
00:01:23,180 --> 00:01:30,900
Its use may be rising, but it is important for us to think of ISAs like programming languages.

20
00:01:30,900 --> 00:01:36,940
That is, learning MIPS or risk five doesn't mean you'll never have to learn another ISA.

21
00:01:36,940 --> 00:01:41,580
And like programming languages, the more ISAs you know, the more prepared you'll be when

22
00:01:41,580 --> 00:01:47,060
you architect one yourself.

23
00:01:47,060 --> 00:01:51,460
We will first look at the basics of a MIPS instruction, followed by registers and arithmetic

24
00:01:51,460 --> 00:01:53,820
ops.

25
00:01:53,820 --> 00:02:03,740
Finally, we'll cover memory and control instructions like branches and jumps.

26
00:02:03,740 --> 00:02:07,780
At this point, I recommend pausing the video and looking at the MIPS green card from the

27
00:02:07,780 --> 00:02:11,140
textbook or the PDF from the course website.

28
00:02:11,140 --> 00:02:16,260
It covers the instructions and syntax for the assembler and will be a handy reference

29
00:02:16,260 --> 00:02:24,860
as we go through this lecture and the rest of the semester.

30
00:02:24,860 --> 00:02:29,860
Take a look at this single statement in C. C enables a programmer to succinctly describe

31
00:02:29,860 --> 00:02:33,620
the mathematical result that they want stored in variable f.

32
00:02:33,620 --> 00:02:39,500
But unlike the complex please x, y, z example earlier, MIPS doesn't have an instruction

33
00:02:39,500 --> 00:02:42,900
that exactly performs this operation.

34
00:02:42,900 --> 00:02:49,500
MIPS can at maximum perform one step with two inputs at a time, so this C statement must

35
00:02:49,500 --> 00:02:54,940
be decomposed into three MIPS instructions.

36
00:02:54,940 --> 00:03:00,020
MIPS has an add instruction that sums two operands and writes to a destination.

37
00:03:00,020 --> 00:03:05,340
We must temporarily store the sums of g plus h and i plus j.

38
00:03:05,340 --> 00:03:10,620
MIPS also has a subtraction instruction that will subtract two operands and store the result

39
00:03:10,660 --> 00:03:16,580
in a destination.

40
00:03:16,580 --> 00:03:24,300
The MIPS ISA assembly syntax specifies that a mnemonic comes first and the operands come

41
00:03:24,300 --> 00:03:26,300
after.

42
00:03:26,300 --> 00:03:32,860
Generally, the outcome comes next, the output, and the inputs are on the right.

43
00:03:32,860 --> 00:03:39,460
Your MIPS green card will tell you when a mnemonic has a different paradigm.

44
00:03:39,460 --> 00:03:47,220
In a load store ISA like MIPS, operands are registers, not variables.

45
00:03:47,220 --> 00:03:53,220
MIPS has 32 word size registers that can be used, 0 through 31.

46
00:03:53,220 --> 00:03:58,860
Now MIPS also specifies label aliases for these register numbers that dictate conventions

47
00:03:58,860 --> 00:04:01,500
that programmers must follow.

48
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:05,340
These labels are also found on the MIPS green card.

49
00:04:05,340 --> 00:04:12,460
In this case, registers T0 and T1 are intended to store temporary variables, and they correspond

50
00:04:12,460 --> 00:04:19,060
to physical registers 8 and 9.

51
00:04:19,060 --> 00:04:24,980
At a high level, our processors 32 registers are used as inputs to the ALU and as outputs

52
00:04:24,980 --> 00:04:27,460
to store.

53
00:04:27,460 --> 00:04:32,380
Some example ALU operations include simple arithmetic like add and subtract, and the

54
00:04:32,380 --> 00:04:37,380
logical operations like and and or or shift left and shift right.

55
00:04:37,380 --> 00:04:42,140
The regular add ADD performs signed arithmetic.

56
00:04:42,140 --> 00:04:46,980
Other flavors will take an immediate or performed unsigned addition.

57
00:04:46,980 --> 00:04:54,380
Note that multiply and divide produces 64 bits of output for more precision, so the results

58
00:04:54,380 --> 00:04:57,500
go into a special double word register.

59
00:04:57,500 --> 00:05:01,620
The MIPS green card will tell you how to load the upper and lower results from the into

60
00:05:01,620 --> 00:05:04,700
your 32-bit register file.

61
00:05:04,700 --> 00:05:06,900
Why do we only have 32 registers?

62
00:05:06,900 --> 00:05:13,140
Well, first off, 32 registers uses less silicon area than 64 or 128.

63
00:05:13,140 --> 00:05:19,340
Second, it takes just 5 bits to specify a unique identifier, and that keeps our instruction

64
00:05:19,340 --> 00:05:20,980
words smaller.

65
00:05:20,980 --> 00:05:27,100
But is 32 registers enough?

66
00:05:27,100 --> 00:05:29,420
Generally no, like with long arrays.

67
00:05:29,420 --> 00:05:33,780
We need to be able to load a register for memory and store a register value back to

68
00:05:33,780 --> 00:05:35,420
memory.

69
00:05:35,420 --> 00:05:40,500
To load a 32-bit word for memory, this load word instruction will cause the address stored

70
00:05:40,500 --> 00:05:44,820
in S1 to be loaded into register T1.

71
00:05:44,820 --> 00:05:51,180
On the flip side, store word will store the value in T1 to the address stored in register

72
00:05:51,180 --> 00:05:52,340
S0.

73
00:05:52,340 --> 00:06:01,940
This is an example of the ISA subtly changing the ordering of source and destination.

74
00:06:01,940 --> 00:06:06,460
Memory typically lives outside the processor chip, though maybe not outside the package

75
00:06:06,460 --> 00:06:14,580
of the processor as 2.5D advanced manufacturing puts multiple chiplets on the same interposer.

76
00:06:14,580 --> 00:06:21,220
Memory is byte-addressed, meaning our variable f is stored in the across addresses 4004,

77
00:06:21,220 --> 00:06:25,820
4005, 4006, and 4007.

78
00:06:25,820 --> 00:06:28,700
Using memory is important for arrays.

79
00:06:28,700 --> 00:06:34,100
Say we need to add h to every index of array a.

80
00:06:34,100 --> 00:06:38,340
Before we get started, I want to highlight the parentheses used in load and store.

81
00:06:38,340 --> 00:06:43,740
S5, by itself, refers to the value stored in register S5.

82
00:06:43,740 --> 00:06:49,660
The parentheses around S5 refer to the value stored in memory at the address contained in

83
00:06:49,660 --> 00:06:51,220
S5.

84
00:06:51,220 --> 00:06:57,780
If this were C, register S5 would contain the pointer, and parentheses S5 parentheses

85
00:06:57,780 --> 00:07:01,260
would contain the value we are loading.

86
00:07:01,260 --> 00:07:07,420
In this example, T0 is our temporary holding spot for the data after a load and before the

87
00:07:07,420 --> 00:07:09,180
store.

88
00:07:09,180 --> 00:07:15,980
Enter S2 contains our constant h, and S5 contains the index multiplied by 4 because we need

89
00:07:15,980 --> 00:07:20,260
a 4-byte word address, not a byte address.

90
00:07:20,260 --> 00:07:25,660
Finally, the address to the zeroth index of a is at 8000.

91
00:07:25,660 --> 00:07:29,460
This could also be called the base address.

92
00:07:29,460 --> 00:07:36,740
Looking at the code, first we load from address 8000 plus the ith word and store it in T0.

93
00:07:36,740 --> 00:07:39,940
Next we add the value of h to T0.

94
00:07:39,940 --> 00:07:43,780
Finally we store T0 back to the same address we loaded it from.

95
00:07:43,780 --> 00:07:46,260
Our update is complete.

96
00:07:46,260 --> 00:07:50,940
MIPS has other load store instructions for bytes and half words should the application

97
00:07:50,940 --> 00:07:54,940
require it.

98
00:07:54,940 --> 00:07:56,500
How do we deal with loops?

99
00:07:56,500 --> 00:08:02,540
In this snippet of C code, the while loop is going to repeat while i does not equal

100
00:08:02,540 --> 00:08:03,940
j.

101
00:08:03,940 --> 00:08:07,420
We don't know how many loops through this code will be required, so let's convert this

102
00:08:07,420 --> 00:08:10,220
to MIPS.

103
00:08:10,220 --> 00:08:17,740
Let's assign i and j to S1 and S2 and assign address labels to the beginning of this while

104
00:08:17,740 --> 00:08:23,620
loop and the location just after the closing scope.

105
00:08:23,620 --> 00:08:28,420
MIPS provide branch instructions that activate when the operands are equal and when the operands

106
00:08:28,420 --> 00:08:29,420
