110
00:08:40,540 --> 00:08:44,100
So our first instruction at the beginning of the loop will branch to the end if we want

111
00:08:44,100 --> 00:08:46,180
to exit.

112
00:08:46,180 --> 00:08:51,540
If we don't take the branch, MIPS will execute the next instruction and stay inside the loop.

113
00:08:51,540 --> 00:08:58,500
At the bottom we'll have an unconditional jump back to the top.

114
00:08:58,500 --> 00:09:01,140
What does branch equal really do?

115
00:09:01,140 --> 00:09:09,820
Well, first it reads the register values in S1 and S2 and then compares.

116
00:09:09,820 --> 00:09:18,340
Depending on the comparison result, it will either set PC to the next word instruction

117
00:09:18,340 --> 00:09:22,020
or it'll set PC to target.

118
00:09:22,020 --> 00:09:28,860
The program counter or PC specifies the address of the currently executing instruction.

119
00:09:28,860 --> 00:09:36,780
To do comparisons other than equal equal or not equal, MIPS provides some pseudo instructions.

120
00:09:36,780 --> 00:09:42,220
For example, branch less than is a pseudo instruction.

121
00:09:42,220 --> 00:09:45,820
What it really does is it executes two instructions.

122
00:09:45,820 --> 00:09:57,660
It sets less than and then it branches not equal to zero.

123
00:09:57,660 --> 00:10:08,620
For MIPS branches, branch equal S1, S2 and immediate allows us to specify the offset.

124
00:10:08,620 --> 00:10:10,580
There's also a branch not equal.

125
00:10:10,580 --> 00:10:16,580
There's set less than, set less than or equal and then also greater than and greater than

126
00:10:16,580 --> 00:10:17,580
equal.

127
00:10:17,580 --> 00:10:21,220
Finally, we have unconditional jumps.

128
00:10:21,220 --> 00:10:24,900
We can just straight up jump to a new address.

129
00:10:24,900 --> 00:10:30,580
We can jump to an address stored in a register and we can jump and link, which is used for

130
00:10:30,580 --> 00:10:32,540
function calls.

131
00:10:32,540 --> 00:10:40,620
Jump and link link first sets the return address to PC plus eight and then it sets the PC to

132
00:10:40,620 --> 00:10:47,260
